<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Cape Breton Island</h1><p class="st-meta">1784 CE – 1820 CE · capital Sydney</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A separate British colony created for Loyalist refugees on Cape Breton Island, later reabsorbed into Nova Scotia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Formerly the French colony of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-royale">Ile Royale</a> with its fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisbourg">Louisbourg</a>, Cape Breton passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1763 and was joined to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>. In 1784, as Loyalists arrived after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> made it a separate colony. Its first lieutenant governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-frederick-wallet-desbarres">Joseph Frederick Wallet DesBarres</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> as the capital in 1785.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was governed by a lieutenant governor and appointed council and never received an elected assembly, which left it unable to levy taxes legally and fed constant quarrels among officials. Its population grew mainly through Scottish Highland immigrants after 1800, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> mines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, leased by the Crown, provided much of the revenue.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Political paralysis and doubts about the legality of its taxes led <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to reannex Cape Breton to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-nova-scotia">Colony of Nova Scotia</a> in 1820, despite local protests. It remains part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1784 CE – 1820 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.20%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1784 CE – 1820 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sydney</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Joseph Frederick Wallet DesBarres</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Presbyterianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Scottish Gaelic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cape Breton</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1802&amp;focus=colony-of-cape-breton-island" aria-label="Colony of Cape Breton Island on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3246 552 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Cape Breton Island lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3566 808L3483 855"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3483 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3566 808h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3566 808h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1802&amp;focus=colony-of-cape-breton-island">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
